        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>How much does the NOHrD Sprintbok cost in the US?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              On 14 October 2026 NOHrD’s US store listed the Sprintbok V2 at $8,199 (Shadow), $8,699 (Oak), $8,999 (Club and Vintage), $9,499 (Cherry) and $10,299 (Walnut). The Sprintbok Pro was $8,899 to $11,099 by finish. Recovery For Athletes, a US dealer, listed the V2 at the same prices.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the weight limit on the NOHrD Sprintbok?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              NOHrD lists a maximum user weight of 160 kg, about 353 lb, and the machine itself also weighs 160 kg. A US dealer and a UK retailer give the same figures.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the difference between the Sprintbok V2 and the Sprintbok Pro?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              They share the frame, belt and ratings. The V2 has a display in the handlebar that syncs with NOHrD’s Connect app; the Pro adds a larger touchscreen with interval, endurance, speed and pacer programmes and scenic routes, for $700 to $900 more in the same finish.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Does the NOHrD Sprintbok need electricity?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The belt does not: it is self-powered and moves only when you push it. NOHrD’s US pages do not state how the console is powered, and a UK retailer lists it as mains powered, so ask before placing it far from an outlet.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What warranty does the NOHrD Sprintbok have?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              NOHrD’s US warranty is one year from initial shipment, upgradeable to two years on all components, including wood, if you register within the first year. Spare parts carry 90 days, and you pay to ship items back on a claim. A UK retailer lists longer terms for its market.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is the NOHrD Sprintbok worth it compared with the AssaultRunner Pro?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              As a running machine they are similar: comparable surfaces and ratings, no motor, no resistance setting. The AssaultRunner Pro costs about $3,499 with a longer frame warranty. The Sprintbok’s extra cost buys wood, craftsmanship and appearance, which is worth it only if those matter to you.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
            database. Where sources disagree we say so rather than pick a number, we say plainly when a
            machine has not been tested hands-on, and we never accept payment for a placement. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Read our review process.</a>
          </p>
